# kubectl apply -f nginx-replicaset.yaml

# Expected output: 
    replicaset.apps/nginx-rs created.

## Step 4 — Watch what happens (open a second Terminal tab)
    In a second Terminal tab, run this — it watches pods in real time:
        
        kubectl get pods -w

    Leave this running. You'll see the 3 pods go from Pending → ContainerCreating → Running live as they appear.


# Step 5 — Inspect everything
    run these one by one and observe the output:
    
    # See the ReplicaSet
        kubectl get rs

    # See the 3 pods it created
        kubectl get pods

    # See pods WITH their labels (notice app=nginx on all of them)
        kubectl get pods --show-labels

    # Full details — notice Events section at the bottom
        kubectl describe rs nginx-rs

    The describe output will show you something like:

        Name:         nginx-rs
        Replicas:     3 current / 3 desired
        Pods Status:  3 Running / 0 Waiting / 0 Succeeded / 0 Failed
        Events:
            Created pod: nginx-rs-xxxxx
            Created pod: nginx-rs-yyyyy
            Created pod: nginx-rs-zzzzz

Step 6 — The self-healing demo!!
    This is the most satisfying part. Delete one pod manually and watch the ReplicaSet immediately create a replacement:


    # Get the exact pod names
        kubectl get pods

    # Delete one (copy a real name from the output above)
        kubectl delete pod nginx-rs-XXXXX

    Switch to your second tab (where kubectl get pods -w is running) and watch — within 2–3 seconds a new pod appears and starts running automatically. The ReplicaSet noticed the count dropped to 2 and brought it back to 3.


# Step 7 — Scale up and down

    # Scale to 5 replicas
        kubectl scale rs nginx-rs --replicas=5

    # Watch the second tab — 2 new pods appear instantly
kubectl get pods

    # Scale back down to 2
        kubectl scale rs nginx-rs --replicas=2

    # Watch — 3 pods are terminated
        kubectl get pods

Step 8 — Try to "trick" the ReplicaSet

    This demonstrates how the selector works. The RS manages pods by their labels — if you add a matching label to a random pod, the RS will adopt it (and may kill one of its own to stay at the replica count).

First, let's run a standalone pod with the same label:

    kubectl run orphan-pod --image=nginx:1.21 --labels="app=nginx"

    kubectl get pods


    This shows the RS doesn't care which pods it manages — it just counts all pods matching its selector.

# Clean up everything 

    # Delete the ReplicaSet (this also deletes all its pods)
    kubectl delete rs nginx-rs

    # Verify everything is gone
        kubectl get pods
        kubectl get rs